<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors of Things that Go – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Red Bike.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Sequence pictures to demonstrate comprehension of texts that include a clear beginning, middle, and end. Reading: Show comprehension of the main action or event in a very simple storybook with pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can sequence pictures to show a clear beginning, middle, and end of the action in a simple story (e.g., "Marta sees the red bus." "The red bus stops." "People exit the bus."). Can show comprehension of the main action or event in a simple storybook by pointing to pictures. Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound /y/ in the word "yellow" when shown a picture of a yellow taxi).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large picture flashcards of colored vehicles (e.g., a red bike, a blue bus, a green car) introduced in Lesson 1. The teacher asks simple questions like "What is this?" and "What color is it?" while pointing to each flashcard, encouraging choral repetition of the words (e.g., "Red bike!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, for instance, a picture of a green car, and says clearly, "This is a green car." Then, the teacher holds up a blue bus flashcard and says, "It is a blue bus." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /k/ in "car" and /b/ in "bus" and /y/ in "yellow" by repeating the words slowly and clearly, and having students imitate the sounds and words. The teacher demonstrates pointing to the specific vehicle or color as mentioned.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple yes/no questions or choice questions while showing two relevant flashcards or pointing to colors.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a red bike and a green car) "Is this a red car?" (Students shake heads or say "No.") "Is this a red bike?" (Students nod or say "Yes.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a bus and a train) "Is it a bus or a train?" (Students point to the correct picture or say "Bus.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated storybook or a set of 3-4 large sequential picture cards depicting a clear beginning, middle, and end of an action involving a colored vehicle (e.g., "Marta sees the red bus," "The red bus stops," "People exit the bus."). Genre: Short narrative sequence. Register: Informal, simple. Format: Picture story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main action or event in a very simple storybook with pictures. Pragmatic: Describing objects using phrases like "It is a red bike" or "It's a blue bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the first picture card and asks, "What do you see?" (Students point and say "Bus!" or "Red bus!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the short sentence corresponding to the picture (e.g., "Marta sees the red bus.") and points to Marta and the bus.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the second picture card and asks, "What happens next?" (Students point to the bus stopping, teacher reads "The red bus stops.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the third picture card, reads the sentence ("People exit the bus."), and asks "Where are the people going?" (Students can point to the people leaving).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After presenting all cards, the teacher shuffles them and asks, "Please, point to the red bus stopping." (Sociolinguistic norm). Students point to the correct picture.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Sequencing pictures to show a clear beginning, middle, and end. Grammar: Color + noun ("green car"), Present simple ("It is a red bike."). Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, train, taxi, red, blue, green, yellow, see, go, stop. Phonemic Awareness: /k/ in "car", /b/ in "bus", /y/ in "yellow". Pragmatic: Answering questions about colors and objects by pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/cloze: Picture-Sentence Match.</b> The teacher displays three large, shuffled picture cards of a simple sequence (e.g., 1. A blue train going. 2. The blue train stopping. 3. People getting off the blue train.). The teacher then reads one simple sentence at a time, for example, "The blue train stops." Students listen and point to the picture that matches the sentence. The teacher ensures students can identify the main action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill: What Happens Next?</b> Students are given a small set of 3 picture cards for a different sequence (e.g., a yellow taxi arriving, stopping, then a person getting out). The teacher shows the first picture and says, "This is a yellow taxi." Then the teacher shows the second picture and asks, "What happens next?" Students are encouraged to point to the action and say one word like "Stop!" The teacher reinforces, "Yes, the yellow taxi stops." (identifying /y/ in yellow). The teacher asks, "Please, point to the yellow taxi."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/peer check: Sequence It!</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of 3-4 shuffled picture cards depicting a simple action sequence (e.g., a green car driving, stopping, then a person waving goodbye). Student A picks up one card and asks, "What is this?" or "What color?" Student B responds by pointing or saying the color/vehicle. Then, Student A attempts to place the cards in order, and Student B helps by pointing and making gestures. The teacher circulates, facilitating and providing support for vocabulary and pronunciation, especially /k/ and /b/ sounds, and confirming the correct sequence.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group activity (3-4 students per group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Each group receives a new set of 3-4 shuffled picture cards illustrating a simple story about a colored vehicle (e.g., "A purple truck drives down the road," "The purple truck stops at the store," "A person loads boxes into the purple truck."). Students must collaborate to arrange the pictures in the correct sequence to tell the story from beginning to end. Once arranged, each student in the group takes turns pointing to a picture and identifying the color and vehicle (e.g., "Purple truck!") or the main action (e.g., "Stop!"). The teacher encourages polite requests like "Please, show the purple truck driving." (Sociolinguistic).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly uses the "Colors of Things that Go" scenario, applying the theme "That's a Red Bike" concepts to other vehicles and colors, requiring students to read and interpret picture sequences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in visually sequencing, demonstrating comprehension of the story's main actions through non-verbal cues (pointing) and single-word utterances. Collaborative interaction within the group to complete the sequencing task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance/Production stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students, in their small groups, present their sequenced picture cards to the teacher. Each student points to one picture in the sequence and uses a gesture or a simple word (e.g., "go," "stop," "see") to indicate the main action or event, demonstrating comprehension of the simple story. They can also point to and identify the colored vehicle in each picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly sequences pictures to show a clear beginning, middle, and end (3 points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of the main action/event through pointing or single words (e.g., "stop", "go", "see") (2 points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to identify colors and vehicles (e.g., "red," "bus") when prompted (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates collaboratively in the group (1 point).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use their fingers to show how well they understood putting the pictures in order: 1 finger (I need help), 2 fingers (I understand a little), 3 fingers (I understand well).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw a simple picture of their favorite colored vehicle from the stories shared today.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for me today when we put the pictures in order? (e.g., "Pointing to the red car.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult for me today? (e.g., "Saying the words.")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully sequence the pictures to show a clear beginning, middle, and end? What common challenges did I observe in picture comprehension or sequencing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students demonstrate comprehension of the main action or event using pointing and simple utterances? Were the phonemic awareness activities (e.g., /k/, /b/, /y/) effective for initial sound identification?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more visual cues, simpler picture stories, repeated modeling) are needed to better support students' reading comprehension and sequencing skills for the next lesson?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use similar sequenced pictures to verbally describe the actions using short sentences (e.g., "The car goes.") and interact with their peers, further practicing the target vocabulary and grammar in an oral communicative context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>